# Windows batch files: cmd.exe REQUIRES CRLF line endings (LF-only .bat files
# mis-parse multi-line if/for blocks -> "was unexpected at this time" crashes).
# text eol=crlf normalizes checkins to LF in the repo and materializes CRLF on
# every checkout, ZIP export and file copy, so future LF-only content edits can
# never be delivered to cmd.exe without CRLF again (the a61f966 regression
# class). The windows-smoke CI step is the behavioral guard for this.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Everything the onboarding router hashes or executes must be LF on EVERY
# checkout. The `sha256:<64 hex>` digests HANDOFF.md records are over the files'
# BYTES, so a CRLF checkout (Git for Windows defaults core.autocrlf=true) makes
# every recorded digest disagree with the file on disk; and a CRLF shell script
# handed to bash dies on "$'\r': command not found". The stored blobs are
# already LF, so pinning eol=lf here changes no content -- it only stops the
# Windows checkout from rewriting it. The behavioral guards are
# tests/test_onboarding_bundle_contract.py (digest equality, byte-exact
# regeneration) and tests/test_onboarding_scripts.py (the scripts actually run).
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
